    { q: "Why does the narrator stop writing about the wallpaper at first?", o: ["She loses her notebook", "John tells her not to give way to fancies", "Jennie takes her pen away", "She finds the subject boring"], a: 1, ex: "يقول جون إنها يجب ألا تستسلم للأوهام، فتحاول أن تكفّ عن الكتابة عنه." },
    { q: "What does the narrator notice about the pattern in moonlight?", o: ["It disappears completely", "It turns green", "It becomes bars with a woman behind them", "It smells of roses"], a: 2, ex: "في ضوء القمر يصير التصميم الخارجي قضبانًا وتظهر خلفه امرأة تهزّها." },
    { q: "Which detail comes first in the story?", o: ["She throws the key into the path", "John chooses the old nursery for her room", "She peels the paper in long strips", "Jennie goes into town"], a: 1, ex: "اختيار جون لغرفة الأطفال القديمة يقع في بداية القصة قبل كل ما ذُكر." },
    { q: "\"It must be very humiliating to be caught creeping in daylight.\" What does humiliating mean?", o: ["making someone feel ashamed", "making someone feel proud", "extremely tiring", "very amusing"], a: 0, ex: "humiliating تعني ما يسبب الذلّ والخجل الشديد." },
    { q: "What does the woman behind the paper most likely represent?", o: ["A real neighbour in the village", "The narrator's own trapped self", "Jennie watching the house", "A childhood memory of the garden"], a: 1, ex: "المرأة المحبوسة خلف القضبان صورة رمزية للراوية نفسها المحبوسة في الغرفة وفي رأي زوجها." },
    { q: "What is the tone of the final paragraph?", o: ["Warm and grateful", "Calmly triumphant and unsettling", "Openly angry and loud", "Playful and light"], a: 1, ex: "تتحدث بهدوء وكأنها انتصرت، وهذا الهدوء نفسه هو ما يجعل النهاية مقلقة." }
